Introduction 

Purpose 

 

This document sets the context and provides guidelines for Design and development of FCB REST APIs only. The content of this document is intended to be used for API team guidance to design and build APIs that are consistent and conform to FCB standards. 

 

Intended Audience 

 

The primary audience for these guidelines are: 

FCB API development and implementation teams 

Architects (Enterprise, Security, Solutions, Integrations, Technical) 

API Product Owners 

Business analyst 

 

Context 

This section sets the context as to what an API is, the type of APIs, and possible interactions with other systems. The context will help the reader understand the rationale for the proposed guidelines in the subsequent sections. The material presented in this section builds material from a variety of resources. The material presented below does not set out to change what an API is from the industry material, but to align it with the FCB’s view of technology. 

 

What is an API? 

 

Application Programming Interface (API) is generally defined as a code-based specification or contract that allows software components to communicate with each other across a network or between two applications running in the same physical memory of a computer.  In non-technical terms, an API is code, which allows an application to communicate with another application or other software components. 

 

What do APIs do? 

 

In the context of this document, APIs serve the purpose of making separate applications work together to produce a unified set of functionalities.  The following describes the type of integration functionality APIs provide: 

 

Application to Application Integration - Passing information or data between two applications within the confines or control of the organization or with the external applications to the organization. This can be a transfer of information between territories or within territories. It could also be an on premise to cloud applications or cloud-to-cloud integration 

Application to Presentation Integration – Exposes information and instructions to the presentation layer. The instruction can be explicit such as snippets of JavaScript executed by the presentation layer or meta-data. Instructions could also consist of navigation in the flow of the subsequent API calls as it maps to a process flow 

 

API Styles and Protocols 

 

The most common web-based APIs are REST, SOAP, JSON-RPC and XML-RPC. 

 

Representational State Transfer (REST) - An architectural style for providing standards between computer systems on the web, making it easier for systems to communicate with each other. REST-compliant systems, often called RESTful systems, are characterized by how they are stateless and separate the concerns of client and server. REST is a great option for implementing CRUD functionality for resources/entities. 

Simple Object Access Protocol (SOAP) - An XML-based messaging protocol for exchanging information among computers. SOAP is an application of the XML specification. Although SOAP can be used in a variety of messaging systems and can be delivered via a variety of transport protocols, the initial focus of SOAP is remote procedure calls transported via HTTP. 

JSON-RPC - A remote procedure call (RPC) protocol which uses JSON to encode its payload data and HTTP as the transport protocol. This is a great option for implementing procedure-based actions. 

XML-RPC - A remote procedure call (RPC) protocol which uses XML to encode its payload data and HTTP as the transport protocol. This is a great option for implementing procedure-based actions.  

 

API Accessibility 

 

Public - Available to systems/users via an endpoint external to the hosting organizations network. Public APIs are sometimes protected by authorization but are always open and do not require a contractual relationship between the client and hosting organization. 

Partner (External) - Available to authorized systems/users (e.g., partners, clients, customers) that are external to an organization’s network. 

Private (Internal) - Available to authorized systems/users that are internal to an organization’s network. 

 

API Function 

In the context of this document, APIs serve the purpose of making separate applications work together to produce a unified set of functionalities.  The following list describes the functionality an API delivers: 

 

Data/System - An interface to access data in a database or system. Typically provides CRUD functionality to the exposed data. Often in integration platforms, APIs such as these are usually referred to as connectors or adapters. 

Business - An interface to business logic and/or business rules that may directly access databases and systems or may leverage data APIs for data access. A business API is sometimes implemented as a composite API. 

Orchestration - An interface to a façade that executes a series of actions or tasks. A process API is typically implemented as a composite API that leverages data and/or business APIs. 

Messaging – An interface to a message broker or similar that supports transporting messages from one system to another. 

 

 

API Types 

The conceptual view of the APIs decomposes into the following types of APIs: 

  

Internal APIs (IA) - Are used for application-to-application integration or as a building block for the APIs to be described below. They are not exposed outside of the firm. These APIs move data between applications, and they could include instructions such as meta-rules around usage or presentation. These APIs should not include code specific instructions such as JavaScript. As mentioned during physical architecture discussions, these APIs could be consumed within a territory, between territories, or globally. 

End User Experience APIs (EUE) - EUEs deal with the presentation layer and the end user experience. For example, an EUE could be tailored to address direct customer interaction or further refined to account for a mobile experience, versus a browser centric experience. EUEs contain information and code to help the presentation layer display the information in the appropriate manner. 

B2B APIs - A B2B API supports information/data sharing between business partners. eg. American Express exposes APIs that the firm calls to pay expenses.  

Product APIs - Product APIs are the APIs used to manage the product catalog and are able to expose information about a product, e.g.	If a deposit account is a product, then a Product API will be able to extract all information about the deposit account. Forrester describes Product API as "increasing product value by connecting to and expanding the ecosystem." In other words, a Product API is directly tied to and part of a product or service an organization sells. 

 

