Security 

 

Guideline: 

 

All APIs must integrate with a Bank-approved Identity Provider (IdP). Okta Enterprise SSO (ESSO) / Okta CIAM is preferred, and integrations must use SAML 2.0 or OIDC/OAuth 2.0. OAuth 2.0 is the preferred method for authenticating and authorizing API calls. Public APIs may only be exposed through an approved API gateway. Transport encryption (e.g., HTTPS) is required for all API traffic. APIs must be inventoried, log security events per IS Logging & Monitoring Standards, protect sensitive data in transit and at rest, and address resource-consumption and injection threats (e.g., rate limiting; input validation/output encoding). CORS must be restricted to trusted origins with explicit allow-lists for origins, headers, and methods. 

 

For example, a client-facing web or mobile app integrates with Okta CIAM using OIDC Authorization Code with PKCE; access tokens are signed, short-lived, audience-restricted, and never stored in browser persistent storage. A backend service-to-service integration uses Client Credentials; secrets are vaulted in a Bank-approved PAM (e.g., CyberArk) and calls may be sender-constrained and constrain is enforced using mTLS. 

 

 

 

FCB uses the following OAuth 2.0 grant types depending on the use case and origin of request: 

 

OpenID Connect Authorization Code (with PKCE) — user flows.  

OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code (with PKCE) — user flows.  

Authorization Code — user flows.  

Client Credentials — non-human (machine-to-machine) flows.  

 

(Authorization Code without PKCE may be permitted for specific user flows; PKCE is preferred.) 

 

Authentication 

 

A Bank-approved Identity Provider (IdP) must be used for all API authentication services.  

Only the following authentication methods are permissible, in order of preference:  

OAuth 2.0 Access Tokens  

Mutual TLS (mTLS) using certificate from Bank-approved CA  

Basic Authentication over encryption channel (as a last resort only and requiring ECSO approval)  

Personal Access Tokens (e.g., for GitHub) must only be employed on an exceptional basis, and with explicit ECSO approval to authenticate 

/authorize user-to-service API calls (i.e., OAuth demonstrated to be non-viable integration method).  

Authentication materials/credentials must be properly secured. OAuth 2.0 and Basic Authentication credentials must be stored in a Bank approved vault (eg, Cyberark/PAM, Apigee) and must not be embedded in code or configuration files. 

 

 

