HTTP Resources 

 

Guideline:  

 

In resource-oriented APIs, resources are named entities.  Collection or Single (rarely used) resource names MUST be based on nouns (a resource/entity) rather than verbs or actions (operations on the resource). Action resource names SHOULD be based on verbs since they will represent operations/actions on the resource. 

 

Examples:  

 

Single Resource - An entity that only has one instance, as qualified in the URL path, such as a user’s profile. 

For example: /party/{partyId}/address, where address is a single resource. 

Single resources are less common than collection resources.  

Only use the singular format when there’s no possibility of the resource having multiples. 

Collection Resource - An entity that can have two or more instances, as qualified in the URL path, such as a user’s messages. 

The entity MUST be expressed as a name and a value. The value MUST NOT be blank. 

For example: /party/{partyId}/identifiers, where persons and identifiers are collection resources. 

Action Resource - Action resources are like procedures/functions typically implemented with RPC. 

The action MUST be expressed as a name and a value. The value MUST NOT be blank. 

A REST API relies on action resources to provide functionality that cannot be logically mapped to one of the standard HTTP verbs. 

Action resources should only be used as an exception. If an API has too many action resources, then it’s likely been designed incorrectly or should be implemented as an RPC API. 

Action resources SHOULD be invoked using the POST verb. 

Action resources SHOULD be identified at the end of the URL, prefixed with an action path segment.  

For example: 

/actions/{action} 

/party/{partyId}/actions/{action} 

