HTTP Request Types 

 

Guideline: 
 

Synchronous 

HTTP is a synchronous transport protocol. 

When an HTTP client issues a synchronous request, it uses the same connection to wait for a response, after the server completes processing the request. 

The preferred request type for GET, HEAD, and any other verbs that can provide timely responses without overloading the server. 

Refer to the HTTP Response Codes section. 

Asynchronous 

When an HTTP client issues an asynchronous request, it receives an immediate response before the server processes the request. 

The preferred request type for transactional verbs (POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE) that cannot be processed in a timely manner or that require significant server resources. 

Allows the client to immediately continue processing rather than waiting. 

Allows a server to throttle its processing load and prioritize processing. 

SHOULD return HTTP status code 202 (Accepted) to indicate the request was accepted for processing but is not completed. 

 

