API Specification 

 

Guideline: 

APIs should be developed using a Top-down approach. Let your API be driven by customer needs. Don't let the back-end systems drive your interfaces. All API Interfaces will be defined using Open API Specifications 3.0 or above. Always follow REST principles when defining your API interfaces. A short list of some things to consider are below: 

Separation of concerns between client and server 

Changes to the client application should not affect the server application and vice versa. The applications must grow and scale independently 

Uniform interface 

Allows client and server to communicate using a single language, independent of the architecture of each of them. It provides standard means of communication by decoupling implementation. Follow naming standards and do not mix different styles in your APIs 

Stateless 

The API should not rely on the server to store the client session data to make a call. The API should provide data in that call itself. To reduce memory, session information is stored in the client (Think local storage for React based applications) 

Client-side caching 

The API should encourage clients to cache data on their side 

Helps reduce number of interactions with the API 

Cache Controls Requirements: <<Add the reference link if exist>>  

Layered system 

Creates scalable, modular applications promoting loose coupling 

Promotes flexibility, and security through multiple layers 

Reduce cost, increase delivery speed and maintainability 

Don’t violate the HTTP specification 

Always use the correct HTTP status code for your operations https://httpstatuses.com/ 

  

