Collections 

 

Guideline: 

APIs that support GETs for collection resources MUST support the following: 

 

Record Selection 

APIs returning more than one record MUST provide the functionality for the caller to step through the result set.  The returning result can be filtered based on querying the resource for a specific record or range of records.  

URL parameters are the easiest way to add basic filtering to REST APIs. If you have an /accounts endpoint which lists the accounts, you can filter via the property name such as 

GET /accounts?partyId=ABC123  

Returns a list of accounts belonging to FCB partyId ABC123. 

 

Sorting 

 

Like filtering, sorting is an important feature for any API endpoint that returns a lot of data. If you’re returning a list of users, your API users may want to sort by last modified date or by email. 

To enable sorting, APIs MUST support sort (or) sort by URL parameter that can take a field name as the value. 

However, good API designs give the flexibility to specify ascending or descending order. Like filters, specifying the order requires encoding three components into a key/value pair. 

Example formats 

GET /transactions?sort=transactionDate 

This returns a list of transactions sorted by descending transaction dates. 

 

Field Selection 

 

Mobile clients display just a few attributes in a list. They don’t need all attributes of a resource. APIs must provide the API consumer the ability to choose returned fields. This will also reduce the network traffic and speed up the usage of the API. 

 

GET /accounts?fields=accountNumber,status,accountType 

 

Paging 

 

APIs returning more than one record MUST provide the functionality for the caller to step through the result like filtering, sorting is an important feature for any API endpoint that returns a lot of data. If you’re returning a list of users, your API users may want to sort by last modified date or by email. 

 

 The API MUST clearly outline the maximum number of records returned in one invocation. 

The return result MUST have a bounding on the number of records returned. The API will exhibit the following behaviors: 

